# 5. COMPUTER VISION SYSTEM

This is one of the most important parts of the project.

Implement actual body pose tracking using:

`@mediapipe/tasks-vision`

Use Pose Landmarker with 33 body landmarks.

Start with the Lite model for performance.

Store the model locally in:

`public/models/pose_landmarker_lite.task`

Avoid unnecessary external runtime dependencies.

## A. Landmark Detection

Use the following landmarks:

| Landmark | Index | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| Right Shoulder | 12 | Right arm reference |
| Right Elbow | 14 | Shooting detection |
| Right Wrist | 16 | Aiming |
| Left Shoulder | 11 | Pause gesture reference |
| Left Wrist | 15 | Pause gesture reference |

Use landmark visibility or presence confidence where available.

Ignore unreliable landmarks.

## B. Aiming Mechanism

The player's right wrist controls the crosshair position.

Implementation:

1. Read the right wrist position from MediaPipe.
2. Use normalized coordinates.
3. Apply initial calibration.
4. Map wrist movement to screen coordinates.
5. Apply smoothing to reduce jitter.
6. Clamp the crosshair to the playable screen area.

The crosshair must respond smoothly without excessive delay.

Use adjustable sensitivity.

Do not directly copy raw wrist coordinates without considering shoulder position, body movement, and camera mirroring.

The preview may be mirrored for a natural user experience, but the coordinate transformation must remain consistent.

## C. Shooting Mechanism

Use the right arm extension gesture to trigger shooting.

Calculate the angle between:

- Right Shoulder.
- Right Elbow.
- Right Wrist.

Initial thresholds:

- Below 130 degrees: Arm is not extended.
- Above 155 degrees: Shooting gesture detected.
- Returning below 130 degrees: Player is ready to shoot again.

These are configurable starting values, not permanent assumptions.

Important requirements:

- One arm extension should produce one shot.
- Do not repeatedly shoot while the arm remains extended.
- Implement cooldown and debounce.
- Require valid landmark confidence.
- Do not shoot when pose tracking is lost.
- Display visual feedback when a shot is registered.

Use 3D world landmarks for joint-angle calculation where practical.

## D. Pause Gesture

Detect both hands raised above shoulder level and held for approximately one second.

Trigger pause only once per gesture.

Prevent accidental repeated activation.

Keep an on-screen pause button as a fallback.

## E. Computer Vision Performance

Implement:

- Pose confidence filtering.
- Smoothing.
- Gesture state management.
- Calibration.
- Tracking-loss detection.
- Recovery when the player returns into frame.
- Inference throttling.
- Separate tracking and rendering update rates.

Target:
- Rendering: approximately 60 FPS.
- Pose inference: approximately 15–20 FPS.

Do not run synchronous pose inference unnecessarily on every Three.js render frame.

If inference causes significant frame drops, consider moving it to a Web Worker.

Provide an optional debug mode showing landmarks, joint angles, crosshair coordinates, and tracking confidence.

